WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.600
Dead bacteria changed living ones into their own type.

2
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.360
What was passed across?

3
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:17.080
In 1928, living harmless bacteria were mixed with heat-killed, deadly ones and

4
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.200
injected into mice.

5
00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:24.920
Some mice died. From them came living bacteria of the dead strain's type, and they

6
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:25.920
bred true.

7
00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:33.973
Later, Avery, MacLeod and McCarty purified the active fraction and tried enzymes on

8
00:00:33.973 --> 00:00:37.993
it. Trypsin and chymotrypsin break down protein.

9
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:43.213
Activity stayed. Ribonuclease breaks down RNA.

10
00:00:44.253 --> 00:00:50.093
Activity stayed. An enzyme preparation that breaks down DNA destroyed it.

11
00:00:50.693 --> 00:00:54.353
They called the agent principally, if not solely, DNA.

12
00:00:54.353 --> 00:00:58.993
A protein below their detection limit could not be ruled out.

13
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:04.907
Phage T2 is little more than a protein coat around DNA.

14
00:01:05.947 --> 00:01:07.267
Protein holds sulfur.

15
00:01:07.887 --> 00:01:10.867
DNA holds none, but is rich in phosphorus.

16
00:01:11.967 --> 00:01:15.987
So Hershey and Chase labelled the protein with sulfur-35.

17
00:01:17.127 --> 00:01:19.947
Phosphorus-32 labelled mostly the DNA.

18
00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:25.227
They let the phage attach to E. coli, then sheared it off in a blender.

19
00:01:25.227 --> 00:01:28.947
About 75% of the sulfur came off.

20
00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:32.107
Only about 15% of the phosphorus did.

21
00:01:32.927 --> 00:01:35.227
The stripped cells still made phage.

22
00:01:35.927 --> 00:01:41.847
Those progeny carried less than 1% of the parental sulfur, and 30% or more of

23
00:01:41.847 --> 00:01:42.847
the parental phosphorus.

24
00:01:43.787 --> 00:01:46.247
This did not prove that no protein entered.

25
00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.810
Then came the structure.

26
00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:53.590
Two chains coil together and run in opposite directions.

27
00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.070
Adenine pairs with thymine.

28
00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:58.590
Guanine pairs with cytosine.

29
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.610
So in double-stranded DNA the amounts match.

30
00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:07.970
If a sample is 22% adenine, thymine is also 22.

31
00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:13.950
That leaves 56% for guanine and cytosine, so each is 28.

32
00:02:14.883 --> 00:02:20.263
Because pairing is specific, one strand fixes the sequence of the other.

33
00:02:21.243 --> 00:02:26.063
Watson and Crick proposed that the two chains separate, and each serves as a

34
00:02:26.063 --> 00:02:27.543
template for a new partner.

35
00:02:28.563 --> 00:02:31.743
Think of each strand as a photographic negative of the other.

36
00:02:32.363 --> 00:02:35.543
Print from each, and you get two copies of the original pair.

37
00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:39.147
That was a proposal, not a test.

38
00:02:39.967 --> 00:02:46.967
In 1958, Meselson and Stahl grew E. coli on heavy nitrogen, then moved it to light

39
00:02:46.967 --> 00:02:50.427
nitrogen. They separated the DNA by density.

40
00:02:51.327 --> 00:02:54.547
After one generation, all the DNA was hybrid.

41
00:02:55.327 --> 00:02:59.807
After two, hybrid and light DNA were present in equal amounts.

42
00:03:00.427 --> 00:03:02.207
That is what templating predicts.

43
00:03:02.207 --> 00:03:08.807
When strands pair by rule, one strand tells you the other, and a gene can be copied.
